WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.180
- I'm taking a couple of
weeks off and while I'm gone,

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:04.520
some brilliant people are
standing in for me here.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.060
First is Alex from Brainbook,
he's a brain surgeon.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.610
There's nothing gruesome in this video,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.710
but there is the answer to a
question I never knew I had,

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:15.900
how do neurosurgeons find their
way around inside the brain?

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.480
- Neurosurgery is fraught with risk.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.300
The brain is packed with
almost 100 billion neurons

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.630
compartmentalised into
complex bundles of nerves

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:27.510
and other structures that
make you who you are.

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.700
Small parts of your brain
are vital for allowing you

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.280
to speak, move, think, learn and love.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.210
As neurosurgeons, we operate in and around

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:37.970
these vital structures and
spend a great deal of time

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:41.190
learning the anatomy so
that we can operate safely.

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:42.870
The room for error can be millimetres

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:46.050
as we manoeuvre around blood
vessels and critical nerves

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.810
and damaging any of these can
cause either life changing

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:51.140
or life ending complications.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.150
No matter how good the
surgeon and no matter

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.820
how good their knowledge,
we can still go astray

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:57.640
and need a helping hand.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.860
Neurosurgery is a
specialty that's inherently

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.180
intertwined with cutting edge technology,

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:03.920
and we can use that to guide us

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.510
when we're navigating the brain.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:07.630
Today I'm going to show
you how neurosurgeons

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:11.800
can use infrared and electromagnetic
image guidance systems

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.160
to show us exactly where
we are in the brain

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.100
during critical operations
like brain tumour surgery,

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.123
or inserting biopsy needles
deep into the brain.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.850
This is a Medtronic StealthStation S8.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:27.130
It's a combination of
hardware and software

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.360
that uses special trackable instruments,

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.753
such as this pointer and this stilette.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.000
To be able to guide the neurosurgeon,

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.800
the system tracks the
position of these instruments

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.270
in relation to the surgical anatomy

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:43.570
and sends that information
to the software.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.740
The software then displays
the instrument's position

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:50.930
on either the CT or MRI
scan of that patient.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.530
The system can track
instruments either optically

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:56.920
using an infrared camera
or electromagnetically.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.650
With optical tracking,
this camera sends out

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:01.490
and then detects infrared light

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:04.210
that's reflected from these silver balls.

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:06.890
The camera then transmits
the instrument's location

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.110
to the navigation software.

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:11.590
Similarly, with electromagnetic tracking,

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:14.410
the emitter emits a low
energy magnetic field

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:17.510
with unique characteristics
at every point.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:20.450
The electromagnetic
instruments contain sensors,

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:22.650
which allow the navigation software

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:27.133
to identify the instrument's location 
within the electromagnetic field.

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:32.590
For the software to display
the instrument's location

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:35.140
in relation to images of
the patient, you've got

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.430
to help the software by creating a map

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:41.400
between points on the patient
and points in the images.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.780
This process is called registration,

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:45.980
and it's essentially the
same for both optical

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:48.110
and electromagnetic types.

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:51.090
After registration is
complete, whenever the surgeon

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:54.740
touches a point on the patient
using one of these tracked instruments,

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.550
the computer uses the map

00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:00.230
to identify the corresponding
point on the images.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:03.130
This identification is called navigation.

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.833
And now I'm going to show
you both systems in action.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.180
Here we've got an MRI
scan of a brain model

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:12.720
that we're going to use.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.510
Coming up, you see this
white blob, which is supposed

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:19.040
to be a simulated brain
tumour on the MRI scan.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.630
Up here, you can see an
eye coming into view,

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:23.100
and then the nose.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.430
So this is the brain that's going to
correspond with the model.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:29.570
In the bottom right hand corner,
we're using infrared tools,

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:33.410
and you can see the pointer
coming into reference

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:36.270
with the reference array
that we've got fixed

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:37.393
to the patient's head.

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.650
We're going to be doing
registration as we mentioned before,

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:44.780
marking out lots of points
with the infrared pointer.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:47.200
Now we can verify the registration.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.280
We've got 1.5 millimetre accuracy,

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.990
which will do for this simulation.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.980
So, we're going to touch the
tip of the nose on the model

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:58.190
and you can see in the MRI
that that correlates well.

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:02.230
Let's look at the inner part
of the eye and that looks good.

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:05.310
The outer part of the eye
and the tip of the ear,

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:06.470
and this is all looking like

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:08.920
it's corresponding really nicely.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.500
We put the pointer into
this hole that's pre made,

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:14.440
we're on the tumour, so
that's looking good as well.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:18.490
And this is a pre made craniotomy
or trapdoor in the skull

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:22.223
that's going to allow us to just
access the tumour straight away.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:26.030
In real life, we'd make this hole

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:29.300
and that would take about
45 minutes to an hour,

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.520
having cut through the skin
and drilled off this bone.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.830
So we can operate around
this tumour and see exactly

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:38.620
where we are in the brain
avoiding critical structures

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:41.750
like blood vessels, nerves
and parts of the brain

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:45.430
that are important for speaking
and seeing, for example.

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:48.810
Now let's move on to the
electromagnetic form of navigation,

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.140
which is also called axiom guidance.

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:54.130
We're going to be using a
Rowena neurosurgical simulator

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:56.540
and thanks a lot to
Susie Glover from Delta

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:59.400
and Stephanie Brown from
NHS Healthcare Horizons.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:03.300
This model is quite cool because it
actually has fluid systems within it

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.750
and here you can see
us actually scanning it

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:09.393
and we're going to take these CT images
and plug them into the software.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.680
Here you can see we've
put the model in pins,

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:15.330
but we don't usually do that
because it can interfere

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.720
with the electromagnetic system.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.330
We're going to be using
this stilette to guide

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:24.100
a piece of tubing deep into
the brain's fluid reservoirs.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:28.480
This pointer is what we're going to use
to do a registration.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:30.940
This is the actual catheter
that we're going to be inserting

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:33.120
deep into the brain and we'll take out

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.830
the little metal stilette
that comes with it,

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:38.733
and insert the tracked axiom stilette

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:42.063
that comes with the
electromagnetic system.

00:05:43.359 --> 00:05:48.220
So now that catheter is
tracked, we can use this pointer

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:51.860
to mark out exactly what
trajectory we're going to be using

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.890
and see where we're going
to need to make a cut

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:57.830
and then later on where we're
going to make a burr hole

00:05:57.830 --> 00:05:59.740
which is a small hole in the skull

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:01.490
that allows us to access the brain.

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:06.370
Now we're going to mark it
with a red marker in this case

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:07.493
and start drilling.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:12.007
[high speed drilling]

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:20.840
Once we've made the hole
and done a bit more work

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:23.720
in real life, we're ready
to put the catheter in.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:26.180
On the right so you can see
that the StealthStation

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:29.460
is showing us how far we need
to go and shows us a target

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:31.070
that we've pre-planned.

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:32.640
We're going to follow that trajectory

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.623
and make sure the green dots line up.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:37.960
As we advance the catheter down,

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:40.500
it gives us a countdown in millimetres.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:43.690
Once we reach zero, we should
be in the ventricle system

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:48.180
and as I take out the metal
stilette from inside the tubing,

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:50.270
fluid should start coming out.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:53.440
And there we are, we are in.
And in real life

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:57.250
we'd now secure this catheter
and get out of there.

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:59.110
- Go and subscribe to Brainbook,
start with his video

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:01.770
on a day in the life of
a neurosurgeon on call.

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:04.723
Next week, we go from brain
surgery to settling on Mars.

